1. READ ONE COMPLETE PRODUCT (apparel item with variants, specs and tags)
   MongoDB : 1 find_one, 1 document returned (3 specs, 10 variants, 2 tags embedded)
   SQL     : 1 statement with 3 LEFT JOINs returns 60 rows (= 3 x 10 x 2 combinations) that the application must de-duplicate
             alternative: 4 separate statements (products, specs, variants, tags)
   observed median per product: MongoDB 3.045 ms (over TCP) | SQLite 0.459 ms (in-process, 4 statements)

2. FILTER ON CATEGORY-SPECIFIC ATTRIBUTES: 5G smartphones, RAM >= 12 GB, price < 30,000
   MongoDB : {'category': 'Smartphones', 'specs.ram_gb': {'$gte': 12}, 'specs.five_g': True, 'price': {'$lt': 30000}}
   SQL     : two self-joins on the EAV table + CAST of TEXT values
   results : MongoDB 1,959 | SQL 1,959  -> MATCH

3. ARRAY ELEMENT FILTER: apparel in size M, Black, in stock
   results : MongoDB 9,569 | SQL 9,569  -> MATCH

4. GROUP-BY REPORT: active products and average price per category
   per-category counts identical: True   (both systems express this easily: $group vs GROUP BY)

5. SCHEMA EVOLUTION: headphones gain 'ip_rating'; products gain 'sustainability' object
   MongoDB : write the new fields; validator allows extra properties; existing documents untouched
   SQL     : either ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN (nullable for every non-headphone row) or new EAV rows;
             a nested 'sustainability' object needs a new table or JSON column
   (SQLite ALTER TABLE ADD COLUMN took 0.6 ms - a metadata change; the cost of schema change is mostly coordination/migrations, not execution time)

